WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
A catalyst can make product appear sooner.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.720
Does that mean there will be more product

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.160
when equilibrium is reached?

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.840
Look at two matching mixtures: the same reacting

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.040
amounts and starting composition, at the same temperature

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.080
and volume.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.560
Both initially form product.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.320
The difference is that only one contains a

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.320
catalyst.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.720
Let us separate the speed of the approach

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.560
from the mixture they approach.

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.566
For a reaction to happen, the reacting particles

00:00:28.807 --> 00:00:31.366
must pass an energy hurdle along a reaction

00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:32.286
pathway.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.326
We call that hurdle activation energy.

00:00:35.687 --> 00:00:38.726
This diagram plots energy against progress along the

00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:40.806
pathway, not against time.

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:44.166
A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a

00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:45.326
lower hurdle.

00:00:45.607 --> 00:00:48.566
At the same temperature, more reacting particles can

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:51.126
use that pathway in a given time, so

00:00:51.287 --> 00:00:52.846
reaction is faster.

00:00:53.207 --> 00:00:56.046
Measure the forward barrier from the reactant level.

00:00:56.327 --> 00:00:58.726
Now measure the reverse barrier from the product

00:00:58.807 --> 00:00:59.126
level.

00:00:59.687 --> 00:01:03.046
Both barriers are lower on the alternative pathway.

00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:05.606
The reactant and product energy levels stay where

00:01:05.687 --> 00:01:06.246
they were.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:10.026
Return to our matching product-forming mixtures.

00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:13.146
Concentration means amount per volume.

00:01:13.627 --> 00:01:16.826
The catalysed mixture makes product faster and approaches

00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:18.586
its equilibrium level sooner.

00:01:19.227 --> 00:01:21.226
Look at the same earlier time on both

00:01:21.307 --> 00:01:24.506
curves: it can already contain more product.

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:27.306
But the two curves approach the same final

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:28.266
concentration.

00:01:28.827 --> 00:01:32.026
The catalyst changes the pathway and speed, not

00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:35.386
the equilibrium composition under these matching conditions.

00:01:35.707 --> 00:01:37.866
These are schematic curves, so they do not

00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:39.946
give exact experimental times.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.686
What if we add the catalyst to a

00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:45.166
mixture already at equilibrium?

00:01:45.247 --> 00:01:47.886
The forward reaction makes product while the reverse

00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:49.566
reaction uses it.

00:01:49.647 --> 00:01:53.006
The catalysed system still has equal average opposing

00:01:53.087 --> 00:01:55.726
rates at equilibrium, so there is no overall

00:01:55.807 --> 00:01:57.566
change in composition.

00:01:57.607 --> 00:02:00.926
Faster continuing reactions do not mean product keeps

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:01.966
accumulating.

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.806
Two closed mixtures start with the same reacting

00:02:05.887 --> 00:02:08.686
amounts and composition, at the same temperature and

00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:09.806
volume.

00:02:09.887 --> 00:02:13.566
Both initially form B as they approach equilibrium.

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:16.126
Only one contains a catalyst.

00:02:16.207 --> 00:02:18.766
Could the catalysed mixture contain more B at

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:20.766
the same earlier time?

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:23.566
After both reach equilibrium, could it contain more

00:02:23.647 --> 00:02:24.366
B?

00:02:24.447 --> 00:02:25.806
Explain the difference.

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:43.953
At an earlier time, the catalysed mixture can

00:02:44.194 --> 00:02:46.993
contain more B because it is approaching equilibrium

00:02:47.154 --> 00:02:50.113
faster from this product-forming starting mixture.

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:53.553
After both reach equilibrium, their composition is the

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:55.553
same under these matching conditions.

00:02:56.034 --> 00:02:59.073
The catalyst speeds the approach; it does not

00:02:59.154 --> 00:03:01.073
give a different equilibrium amount.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.180
A useful memory anchor is speed, not the

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.940
equilibrium destination.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.580
Use it with the matching conditions we stated.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.740
A lower alternative pathway helps the opposing reactions,

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.380
and the mixture approaches equilibrium faster.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.900
More product during a limited time is different

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.820
from more product at equilibrium.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.220
Next, the equilibrium-expression lesson will give a mathematical

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.380
way to describe that final mixture.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.380
Here are the useful notes.
